A single process runs the following C fragment. Every call to `fork()` succeeds.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
    fork();
```

How many **new** (child) processes are created in total?

Consider the following program. Every `fork()` succeeds, returning 0 in the child and a positive PID in the parent. C short-circuit evaluation applies to `||`.

```c
int main(void) {
    if (fork() || fork())
        fork();
    printf("x\n");
    return 0;
}
```

How many times is `x` printed?

Two processes $P_0$ and $P_1$ share `boolean flag[2] = {false, false};`. Process $P_i$ (with $j = 1 - i$) runs:

```c
while (true) {
    while (flag[j]) ;      /* busy wait */
    flag[i] = true;
    /* critical section */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Which statement is correct?

Two processes share `int turn = 0;`. Process $P_i$ (with $j = 1 - i$) runs:

```c
while (true) {
    while (turn != i) ;    /* busy wait */
    /* critical section */
    turn = j;
    /* remainder section */
}
```

Which requirement of the critical section problem is **not** satisfied?

A shared integer `x` is initialised to 1. Two threads run concurrently. Each statement is compiled to three steps: load `x` into a private register, compute, store the register back to `x`. Steps of different threads may interleave in any order.

```c
/* Thread A */          /* Thread B */
x = x * 2;              x = x + 3;
```

How many **distinct** final values of `x` are possible?

A bounded buffer with $N$ slots uses semaphores `mutex = 1`, `empty = N`, `full = 0`. The code is:

```c
/* Producer */                 /* Consumer */
wait(mutex);                   wait(full);
wait(empty);                   wait(mutex);
  insert item;                   remove item;
signal(mutex);                 signal(mutex);
signal(full);                  signal(empty);
```

What can go wrong?

Semaphores `a` and `b` both start at 0. Three processes run concurrently, each executing its statements in order. `print` is atomic.

```c
/* P1 */          /* P2 */          /* P3 */
print("A");       wait(a);          print("D");
signal(a);        print("C");       wait(b);
print("B");       signal(b);        print("E");
```

How many distinct 5-character output strings are possible?

Consider the program below. Assume `fork()` and `execlp()` both succeed and that `ls` exits normally.

```c
int main(void) {
    if (fork() == 0) {
        execlp("ls", "ls", NULL);
        printf("child\n");
    }
    printf("done\n");
    return 0;
}
```

How many times is `done` printed?

Every `fork()` in the program below succeeds. `continue` jumps to the next loop iteration (after `i++`) and `break` leaves the loop.

```c
int main(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        if (fork() == 0) {          /* child */
            if (i % 2 == 0) continue;
            else break;
        }
    }
    printf("*");
    return 0;
}
```

How many `*` characters are printed in total (counting all processes)?

Consider the program below. All system calls succeed.

```c
int fd[2];
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0) {                    /* child */
    char buf[64]; int n;
    while ((n = read(fd[0], buf, sizeof buf)) > 0)
        write(1, buf, n);
    printf("child done\n");
    exit(0);
}
close(fd[0]);
write(fd[1], "hello", 5);
close(fd[1]);
wait(NULL);
printf("parent done\n");
```

Note that the child never closes its copy of `fd[1]`. What happens?

Every `fork()` succeeds and every `printf` writes its output immediately (no buffering).

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    fork();
    printf("%d", i);
}
```

How many characters are printed in total?

Consider two programs. In both, `x` is a global `int` initialised to 5 and all calls succeed.

```c
/* Program A */
int main(void) {
    if (fork() == 0) { x = x + 10; printf("%d ", x); exit(0); }
    wait(NULL);
    x = x * 2; printf("%d\n", x);
}

/* Program B */
void *f(void *arg) { x = x + 10; printf("%d ", x); return NULL; }
int main(void) {
    pthread_t t;
    pthread_create(&t, NULL, f, NULL);
    pthread_join(t, NULL);
    x = x * 2; printf("%d\n", x);
}
```

What do the two programs print?

A monitor uses Mesa semantics (the signaller continues; the woken thread only becomes ready). Several consumer threads share it.

```
monitor Buffer {
    int count = 0;
    condition notEmpty;
    void put(item v) { /* store v */ count++; notEmpty.signal(); }
    item get() {
        if (count == 0) notEmpty.wait();
        count--; /* remove and return an item */
    }
}
```

Which problem can occur?

Shared variables: `int x = 0;` and a counting semaphore `S = 2`. Three processes P1, P2, P3 each run the code below, where `r` is a private register. Each numbered line is atomic, and the processes may interleave in any way.

```c
for (int k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
    wait(S);
    r = x;          /* line 1 */
    x = r + 1;      /* line 2 */
    signal(S);
}
```

What is the minimum possible value of `x` after all three processes finish?

$n$ threads share `int count = 0;` and semaphores `mutex = 1` and `barrier = 0`. Each thread runs the following once:

```c
wait(mutex);
count = count + 1;
if (count == n) signal(barrier);
signal(mutex);
wait(barrier);       /* turnstile */
signal(barrier);
/* point X */
```

